th} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386454} {:answer/id #uuid "a98c0a47-fad4-54bd-9a08-1016c8c5e268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386455} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e5851d-1f19-524f-9845-a03add07f035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386456} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4a0c0b-1762-5e1b-a78b-303307949962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386457} {:answer/id #uuid "235fcb2d-aad6-533c-8d07-75611f87ebf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "382f6341-07e9-5d75-adb4-5a4fcdfe016a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386454} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4f5269a-bb75-592f-9f58-18875884cd1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a37675f-e872-5fca-bf67-03df4f2e5cdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-109741" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $S$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(e-\\lambda )^{5}(\\lambda +\\sqrt{3})^{4}(5-\\lambda )^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{16}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $S,$ how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(e-\\lambda )^{5}(\\lambda +\\sqrt{3})^{4}(5-\\lambda )^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =e$ has multiplicity $5.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e$ is $5.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =e$ is $5-2=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d41baa7-2f99-5aa9-9e20-9c3e4ee48d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490534} {:answer/id #uuid "7112d1d7-2482-5dbe-9b34-f2f88cda3ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490733} {:answer/id #uuid "a1eb0cde-a682-5537-836e-88895a46e126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490842} {:answer/id #uuid "b461c956-eb03-540c-937a-5468ab8906b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490870} {:answer/id #uuid "d859ee8e-2941-509f-9843-675fc04e101f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1eb0cde-a682-5537-836e-88895a46e126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490842} :answer-field/id #uuid "13419c69-6e14-595f-b394-69e8b9ff2c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccaaf964-e8e6-5357-a7a2-fd338a200e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-109743" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{2}(\\lambda +11)^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there is exactly $1$ eigenvector. If a basis of ${R}^{13}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{2}(\\lambda +11)^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $4.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $4.$\n\nHowever, there is only $1$ eigenvector corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $1.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $4-1=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c4a71d-0628-5ff8-8f7b-d46974a371a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490583} {:answer/id #uuid "4a251038-f751-5a21-aad4-7863df5e48fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490652} {:answer/id #uuid "6b88a4da-cde6-54e9-abf5-e9ffa5b03287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490719} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee85073-8e29-5a00-b49d-ca1b9acce08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490763} {:answer/id #uuid "966b8de0-1dc1-520c-ae6b-f1958e5ed590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ee85073-8e29-5a00-b49d-ca1b9acce08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490763} :answer-field/id #uuid "1df4e2b8-e955-5de3-92b7-c0a2fa78d648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d072dedc-173a-5c05-84bc-fb318762eb6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109746" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda -3)^{6}(\\lambda +2)^{3},$ the nullity of $(A-3I)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(A-3I)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-3I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-3I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-3I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-3I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(A-3I)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7850b20a-981a-5296-97b3-dc8c88b418f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490750} {:answer/id #uuid "9df0b47f-d9ba-5e21-ab5c-846c6841d780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490837} {:answer/id #uuid "b4998b58-9a1f-567b-b53e-6bfb546a1bf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490871} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7f06db-99e8-5950-b916-b449dc2e08fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490955} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8a339c-48e6-5319-a41f-01bfb9b556a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8a339c-48e6-5319-a41f-01bfb9b556a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490995} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2124c99-e04c-5d5a-81fa-ec9469dfdbf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6337c5ef-f189-51fa-920d-63ed0153be2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109752" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(3-\\lambda )^{2}(\\lambda -\\sqrt{2})^{15}(\\lambda -3)^{4},$ the nullity of $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ equals $2,$ and the nullity of $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{21}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $C$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2},$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}=4-2=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f71f7292-bd10-52a5-b0a7-f6ea545eff02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375722} {:answer/id #uuid "0925206f-5716-5628-8e80-af76824fd5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375723} {:answer/id #uuid "978a317f-5603-5b4d-b70c-bb75018431e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375724} {:answer/id #uuid "073891a0-1fd5-5d8b-9990-d4208d56e2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375725} {:answer/id #uuid "543cbc6b-d49e-5c0e-83cb-83010e06d3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "978a317f-5603-5b4d-b70c-bb75018431e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375724} :answer-field/id #uuid "3538ec67-039b-5162-a8f1-3e30a944e77d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fab609bc-c26d-54d9-ae3c-82ed6883e1ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-109840" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $S$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(7+\\lambda )^{3}(\\lambda -\\sqrt{3})^{4}(\\lambda +2)^{3},$ the nullity of $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{10}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $S,$ how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $S$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3},$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f99e9009-e6c2-582d-90ae-d0bfcf1f5d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362725} {:answer/id #uuid "99d135b5-1334-5d73-b629-c04ae8d41c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362726} {:answer/id #uuid "2dac6776-db44-5e53-8d0e-7467411d2060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362727} {:answer/id #uuid "ac89cd1b-b62b-50dd-b0a5-0452ce5ed0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362728} {:answer/id #uuid "34ffef47-4e58-50ef-b079-896389f7f959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f99e9009-e6c2-582d-90ae-d0bfcf1f5d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362725} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd562744-db8f-593a-9c8b-77f2c75d981e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27ab5139-4b6b-5639-b90d-136b8a14f7bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109843" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(\\lambda +1)^{16}(\\lambda -8)^{2},$ the nullity of $(A+I)^{4}$ equals $4,$ and the nullity of $(A+I)^{5}$ equals $7.$ If a basis of ${R}^{20}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A+I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A+I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$ has dimension $7.$ This means that the solutions to $(A+I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $7.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $4$ in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{4}=7-4=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94209dd4-8332-5a59-a665-1704486cfa93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175553} {:answer/id #uuid "a5527931-a3b1-55fa-9b63-7717e8e64f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175554} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa7a214-ba76-554e-beac-ba26ce0b267b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175555} {:answer/id #uuid "556528e8-e14c-5170-a694-d9d52bcbb8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175556} {:answer/id #uuid "7cda7705-15f5-55b4-9121-da930449a99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cda7705-15f5-55b4-9121-da930449a99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175557} :answer-field/id #uuid "24a6c438-eab2-5a25-b355-d190884b3e3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e04a0ca0-a400-5c37-86e4-06c47bca3608" :question/math-academy-id "q-78160" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=\\lambda (\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -2)^{4},$ the rank of $(A-2I)^{2}$ equals $7,$ and the rank of $(A-2I)^{3}$ equals $6.$ If a basis of ${R}^{8}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-2I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be an $8\\times 8$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-2I)^{2}$ equals $7,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{2}=8-7=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}$ of dimension $1$ in ${R}^{8}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-2I)^{3}$ equals $6,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{3}=8-6=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-2I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$ of dimension $2$ in ${R}^{8}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $1$ in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $2,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{2}=2-1=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e865e05-d063-5259-9427-8e9ac3415420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364422} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d54eb7-8c94-5ba1-8ff1-0e33e8e8ff4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364423} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca18b90-c676-50ef-bb00-24c4e41c4b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364424} {:answer/id #uuid "61161b93-ea6b-5325-aa7b-d6e1119b0a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364425} {:answer/id #uuid "1c60cb57-ff4f-5029-8ce2-b8fe29a39c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e865e05-d063-5259-9427-8e9ac3415420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364422} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b1e17b-e37b-5c86-92c1-41321a65eb42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "246e62ce-be4d-58bc-8aa3-d8a9e5b8b661" :question/math-academy-id "q-78463" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(1+\\lambda )^{4}(1-\\lambda )^{2}(4-\\lambda )^{3},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ there is exactly $1$ eigenvector. If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(1+\\lambda )^{4}(1-\\lambda )^{2}(4-\\lambda )^{3},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $4.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $4.$\n\nHowever, there is only $1$ eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $1.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1$ is $4-1=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2a744a-7e8d-5a8d-8a93-dc76818c873e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490527} {:answer/id #uuid "13a5a88c-0b5a-5a89-b737-dc9f96f87647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490550} {:answer/id #uuid "6a001863-7677-56f5-a48e-46de61c50db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490712} {:answer/id #uuid "76c17061-49ab-5782-90a2-1a974e55e8e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490746} {:answer/id #uuid "f5678664-def6-56da-8fb7-91cc385210a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5678664-def6-56da-8fb7-91cc385210a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490981} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f04c93-2db7-56ff-bf49-28c30a11d6a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "247f09ea-5442-5e08-b3bd-d68a92ad4690" :question/math-academy-id "q-78478" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda -7)^{12},$ the rank of $(A-7I)^{5}$ equals $3,$ and the rank of $(A-7I)^{4}$ equals $5.$ If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-7I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14\\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-7I)^{4}$ equals $5,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{4}=14-5=9.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-7I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}$ of dimension $9$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-7I)^{5}$ equals $3,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{5}=14-3=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-7I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$ of dimension $11$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $9$ in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $11,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{4}=11-9=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$"}]}
